# Use the right tone

<h2 id="speak-to-the-household-directly">Speak to the household directly</h2>

Say “you” and say who the message is from.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The user’s heating has been turned off. | Your heating is off. Home Assistant turned it off at 22:00. |

<h2 id="wg-011">Match certainty to evidence</h2>

Say what was observed. Do not turn an old reading or a request into a fact.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The front door is locked. | The front door lock reports Locked. |

<h2 id="wg-012">Be calm without false reassurance</h2>

Give the fact and a next step. Do not joke, blame the device or say “do not worry”.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Oops! Your pesky lamp is being stubborn. Just try again! | The lamp has not reported its new state. Check the lamp, then try again. |

<h2 id="wg-046">Do not blame or raise false alarms</h2>

Do not blame the person. Keep alarm wording for real alarms, because too many urgent messages teach people to ignore them.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| You left the window open again. | The bedroom window sensor reports Open. It has been open for 40 minutes. |
| URGENT: Front door lock battery critical!!! | Front door lock battery at 20%. Replace it soon. |

<h2 id="wg-066">Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain</h2>

A tracker reports a device, not a person. Hide presence on shared screens unless the household chose to show it.

<%= example('wg-presence') %>

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Alex is out. House empty. | Alex’s phone last reported Away at 17:40. |
